From: canoziia <canoziia@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] qwen3.8: MTP draft widths - draft k, verify exactly those k

VLLM_QWEN38_VERIFICATION_WIDTHS (default 0 = unchanged): when the scheduler
schedules a batch it decides how many drafts k the last PP rank generates for
it (SchedulerOutput.qwen38_next_draft_k). Every scheduled request records
qwen38_drafted = k, async placeholders are exactly k, and the request's next
verification consumes exactly the drafts that were generated (a manual
vllm_xargs.spec_k caps it; preemption resets it). spec_k is parsed, validated
and turned into the limit in vllm/v1/core/sched/qwen38_spec_k.py, reached only
from two Qwen3.8-architecture-gated call sites: InputProcessor._validate_params
(HTTP 400) and Scheduler.add_request. The generic request model and
SamplingParams are untouched; other models ignore the key as before (JSON
booleans arrive as 0/1 after the generic coercion).
The MTP drafter runs its position-0 routine plus k-1 fused decode
steps; one fused draft-decode CUDA graph is captured per step count, and exact
uniform target decode graphs are captured for every verification width 1+k.
One count per scheduled batch keeps both PP stages on the same exact graph; the
draft feedback broadcast keeps its [reqs, K] shape (positions >= k are never
consumed). Without the policy every batch drafts K (manual spec_k aside).

VLLM_QWEN38_ADAPTIVE_WIDTH (default 0; needs the above): the DeepSeek V4.1
measured verification policy (adaptive/0009) ported to MTP k<=K chooses k per
request: per-position at-risk acceptance, 0.95 decay, measured output/time
blended with one calibrated predicted block from Qwen's measured cost of
drafting and verifying k, 98 %-of-peak cold choice with +3 %, then +3 % plus a
ratio/paired-prefix uncertainty gate, at most one width of growth per decision,
manual spec_k first, failure keeps K. One count per batch: between the
smallest and largest member choice, the width with the highest summed relative
policy score (a single request gets exactly its choice). Growing the width
means drafting more next time; no full-width proposal is generated or retained.
